{
  "dom_id": "step-q88306",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "$|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
